// @NAME: Getting Started
// @TITLE: Put a wall between two hexes, bounce off its exact surface, and sweep a body into it.
//
// `hex_edge` is collision on the hex lattice.  A wall sits BETWEEN two cells, so it is stored
// on the EDGE they share, in a `hex_field::EdgeSet`.  Each marked edge carries the id of an
// analytic SURFACE — a straight line or an arc — so a body that hits it gets the wall's exact
// normal, not one of the six directions a hex edge happens to face.  Each edge can also carry
// a MATERIAL: how tall, how opaque, how loud.
//
// It is not the cell set or its outlines — that is `hex_field`, which also owns the `EdgeSet`
// storage.  It is not coordinates and neighbours either: that is `hex_grid`.  Reach for
// `hex_edge` when something has to stop, bounce off, or see past a wall.
//
// Cells are `(q, r)` in odd-r offset coordinates; a cell's centre is at
// `(lattice_k(q, r) · √3/2, lattice_m(q, r) / 2)`, so neighbouring centres are √3 apart.
use hex_edge::*;
use hex_field::*;

const SQ3 = 1.7320508075688772;

fn main() {
  e = edgeset_new(-4, -4, 9, 9);          // a 9 x 9 chunk around the origin
  edge_block(e, 0, 0, 1, 0);              // a wall between (0,0) and its east neighbour
  println("east blocked {!passable(e, 0, 0, 1, 0)}, from the other side {!passable(e, 1, 0, 0, 0)}, north-east open {passable(e, 0, 0, 0, -1)}");
  assert(!passable(e, 0, 0, 1, 0) && !passable(e, 1, 0, 0, 0), "one edge, asked from either side");
  assert(passable(e, 0, 0, 0, -1), "the cell's other edges are untouched");

  // Every section below is a function, and `main` calls each one: a section nothing calls
  // still compiles, but its assertions never run.
  a_wall_knows_its_surface();
  sweeping_a_body();
  what_a_wall_is_made_of();
}

// That prints:
//
//     east blocked true, from the other side true, north-east open true
//
// ## A wall knows its surface
//
// `edge_block` marks an edge with no geometry: it blocks, but `collide` answers a normal of
// (0, 0), and reflecting a velocity off that changes nothing.  Give the wall a surface instead
// and `collide` answers the exact normal.  Surface id 0 is reserved for "no surface", so the
// first surface you add is id 1.  `edge_point` is where the edge sits — the point the normal
// is evaluated at.

fn a_wall_knows_its_surface() {
  s = surfaces_new();
  (px, py) = edge_point(0, 0, 1, 0);
  assert(px == SQ3 / 2.0 && py == 0.0, "half way between the two centres");
  wall = surf_straight(s, 1.0, 0.0, px);       // the line x = √3/2, facing +x
  assert(wall == 1, "id 0 is taken");

  bare = edgeset_new(-4, -4, 9, 9);
  edge_block(bare, 0, 0, 1, 0);
  (b1, nx1, ny1) = collide(bare, s, 0, 0, 1, 0);
  assert(b1 && nx1 == 0.0 && ny1 == 0.0, "blocked, and no direction to bounce in");

  known = edgeset_new(-4, -4, 9, 9);
  edge_block_surf(known, 0, 0, 1, 0, wall);
  (b2, nx2, ny2) = collide(known, s, 0, 0, 1, 0);
  assert(b2 && nx2 == 1.0 && ny2 == 0.0, "the wall's own normal");
  println("surface : bare wall ({nx1}, {ny1}), surfaced wall ({nx2}, {ny2})");
}

// ## Sweeping a body
//
// `sweep_path(e, x0, y0, x1, y1)` walks a segment edge by edge and stops at the first marked
// one.  It answers `(t, q, r, dir)`: the fraction travelled, the cell it stopped in, and the
// direction of the wall — `dir < 0` when nothing blocked.  There is no step size, so a fast
// body or a dropped frame cannot jump a wall.
//
// Centre to centre across the wall, the stop is exactly half way.  That stop lies ON the line
// between the two cells, and there `hex_at` names the FAR cell.  So a second `sweep_path`
// from the stop starts past the wall and walks straight through.  The sweep already told you
// the cell you are in: pass it back with `sweep_path_from`, and pressing on travels nothing.

fn sweeping_a_body() {
  e = edgeset_new(-4, -4, 9, 9);
  edge_block(e, 0, 0, 1, 0);
  (t, cq, cr, d) = sweep_path(e, 0.0, 0.0, SQ3, 0.0);
  assert(t == 0.5 && cq == 0 && cr == 0 && d == 0, "stopped half way, in (0,0), facing east");

  sx = t * SQ3;
  (t2, q2, r2, d2) = sweep_path_from(e, cq, cr, sx, 0.0, SQ3, 0.0);
  assert(t2 == 0.0 && q2 == 0 && r2 == 0 && d2 == 0, "threaded: still against the wall");
  (t3, _q3, _r3, d3) = sweep_path(e, sx, 0.0, SQ3, 0.0);
  assert(t3 == 1.0 && d3 < 0, "re-derived: the far cell, and the wall is behind it");
  println("sweep   : stop at {t}; threaded {t2} further, re-derived {t3} (through the wall)");
}

// ## What a wall is made of
//
// A `Materials` palette gives each edge a substance: solid or not, height, opacity, sound
// damping in dB, permeability and bounce.  `edge_block_full` marks the edge with a surface
// and a material together.  Material id 0 is "nothing there", and every reader answers a
// neutral value for it — 0 height, but permeability 1.0, fully open.
//
// `sight_clear` reads opacity and height, so a solid but transparent fence stops a body and
// not a look.  Movement reads NO material: `passable` and `sweep_path` stop at any marked
// edge.  So `material_set_solid(m, gate, false)` does not open a gate — clearing the edge's
// surface mark does.

fn what_a_wall_is_made_of() {
  s = surfaces_new();
  wall = surf_straight(s, 1.0, 0.0, SQ3 / 2.0);
  m = materials_new();
  stone = material_add(m, true, 2.0, 1.0, 40.0, 0.0, 0.2);   // solid, 2 tall, opaque
  fence = material_add(m, true, 2.0, 0.0, 3.0, 1.0, 0.0);    // solid, see-through
  assert(stone == 1 && fence == 2, "id 0 is taken here too");
  assert(mat_height(m, 0) == 0.0 && mat_perm(m, 0) == 1.0, "nothing there: no height, fully open");

  g = edgeset_new(-4, -4, 9, 9);
  edge_block_full(g, 0, 0, 1, 0, wall, stone);
  f = edgeset_new(-4, -4, 9, 9);
  edge_block_full(f, 0, 0, 1, 0, wall, fence);
  // an eye at height 1 in (0,0), looking at height 1 into (1,0)
  assert(!sight_clear(g, m, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0, SQ3, 0.0, 1.0, 0.5), "stone hides it");
  assert(sight_clear(f, m, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0, SQ3, 0.0, 1.0, 0.5), "the fence does not");
  assert(!passable(f, 0, 0, 1, 0), "but the fence still stops a body");

  material_set_solid(m, stone, false);
  assert(!mat_solid(m, stone) && !passable(g, 0, 0, 1, 0), "a material change moves nothing");
  edge_set_surf(g, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0);
  assert(passable(g, 0, 0, 1, 0), "clearing the mark opens it");
  println("material: stone hides, the fence does not; a gate opens by clearing its mark");
}

